WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.696 --> 00:00:09.870
- All right, great.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:13.130
Thank you so much for the
invite to Peter and the DHLab.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:18.130
I'm here on behalf of Melissa
Grafe and Arthur Belanger

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:20.490
at the Medical Heritage Library

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.430
and the Cushing/Whitney
Medical Library here at Yale.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.530
And I'm gonna talk about part of a project

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.640
I helped out with this past summer

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.847
and that's been kind of
humming away on our workstation

00:00:31.847 --> 00:00:34.323
through the fall and the winter.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.860
So my title is Mining the
Medical Heritage Library,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:42.320
Notes towards a lightweight,
open machine vision solution.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.580
And here's a quick agenda.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.760
I want to talk about what MHL is.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.040
And then the task that
I was mainly working on,

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.900
which was eliminating
false-positive or junk,

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.910
non-illustrations that
MHL was trying to upload

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.654
to their Flickr account in their quest to

00:00:56.654 --> 00:01:00.520
upload centuries of medical illustrations.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.670
Talk about the solution
that I helped come up with,

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:08.560
how it uses deep rooting
techniques, some results,

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:09.930
and then some next steps

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.900
on what I've currently been
working with frameworks

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.350
for sort of collaborative annotation

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.580
and forward annotation of images.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.293
I want to talk a lot
about the importance of

00:01:19.293 --> 00:01:24.293
using training data in a
reproducible and transparent way.

00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:26.720
So a lot of the talks, I've talked about

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.190
their sort of motivating challenges.

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:30.580
So I thought I'd want to do that as well.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.883
The preliminary work of data collection,

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.280
especially when you're thinking
about small organizations

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.180
or individual researchers, is very hard.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.450
It's sort of thankless.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:44.050
It doesn't seem to connect to scholarship

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.650
or be professionally
legible in many cases.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.500
And this is especially true when the data

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.220
you're trying to get, you have
to download over a network.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.250
For instance, in our case it
existed on Internet Archive.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.763
And you don't already
have it lying around.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:00.580
And then you'll do some
training in the model.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:01.610
You'll create.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.890
You might save for a
while, put on a model zoo,

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.010
as they're called.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.840
But the data you actually
use to train that model

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.256
is often forgotten or
thrown out once you've hit

00:02:11.256 --> 00:02:13.389
an arbitrary accuracy goal, or more often

00:02:13.389 --> 00:02:17.390
when you're just left that project behind.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.950
So I wanna think about
machine vision solutions

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:24.300
for small organizations
and individual researchers,

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.870
for instance PhD students like myself.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.310
What can you do if you need to move fast

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.650
and you don't have a bunch
of institutional funding

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.700
and you maybe even hack something together

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.790
with one collaborator, by yourself,

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:37.580
over the course of a few weeks,

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.260
using one of these
public digital libraries

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.503
or resources like the Internet Archive?

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.020
Now there's a lot of tutorials

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.560
and models, and frameworks out there,

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.670
more than ever driven by
entrepreneurial forces and industry

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:53.470
And the kind of payment
tiering for all this

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:54.303
is still emerging.

00:02:54.303 --> 00:02:55.370
So I'll try to share some notes

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.463
on what I found worked for me.

00:02:58.718 --> 00:03:00.040
And then, okay, what's the data?

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:04.010
We've seen a lot of awesome
natural science data.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.080
Well the data I was working with,

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.760
with MHL was books,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.440
which are very messy,

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.750
even more so than
newspapers and periodicals.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.990
But they're also incredibly structured

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.590
and very semantically rich and
predictable in certain ways

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.960
that we can take advantage of.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.260
And then a thought about book data

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.310
is whether it's better
to curate sets of books

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:30.190
and have very rich annotations
and structure for them,

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:33.190
or to make tools that
work with the existing

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:34.540
major digital libraries

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:37.550
like Internet Archive,
HathiTrust, and so forth.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:41.590
And then in this area of
extracting and servicing

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:44.330
historical illustrations,
kind of find people

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.940
that are also interested in this,

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:47.820
or may become interested when they find

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.373
that this archive may
pertain to their own work.

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.560
Okay, so what is the
Medical Heritage Library?

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.570
Well, it was founded in 2010.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.990
And it's a consortium of
different medical libraries

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:00.670
and special collection center,

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:04.880
among them Yale, Harvard,
National Library of Medicine,

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.830
institutions like the Wellcome
in London and in France.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:13.830
And it spans basically the
16th century to the present.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:16.280
And it's a digital consortium,

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:18.880
so it has an org type,

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.390
but it mainly exists on Internet
Archive as a collection.

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:26.030
So here it is and there's
almost 300,000 volumes now.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:27.610
All of them have been digitized

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:29.840
by the various partner libraries,

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.020
and scanned and put on Internet Archive

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.360
where anyone can download them as PDFs,

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.010
or using Internet Archive's APIs,

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.220
they can download them in
bulk if they have enough time

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.010
and enough space.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:44.023
So MHL has a few tools.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:46.670
The Conway Library at
Harvard has developed

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:49.020
a nice full text searching tool

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.630
that gives you a bit more capability

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:53.250
than Internet Archive itself has.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:56.883
But what MHL approached me
about was their Flickr project.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.300
They are extracting these illustrations

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:04.520
from these historical
medical treatises, pamphlets,

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.380
medical journals and posting
them so that the public

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.990
can view them on Flickr.

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:11.130
Whether or not this is
the best place to do it,

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.307
that's sort of beside the point.

00:05:12.307 --> 00:05:13.960
One of the points of this presentation

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.290
is if you're given a task like this,

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:19.140
how can you best use what's
out there to make it work?

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.410
So MHL's Flickr accounts,

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:24.650
one of them with all this
noisy junk that I'll show you.

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:28.650
So these are all images
that in the pipeline

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:32.170
they were using were
deemed to be illustrations

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:34.440
or to have pictorial content.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.210
These are probably from
19th century books.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:39.500
As you can see, a lot of tabular data,

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.190
a lot of textual data was incorrectly

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:45.480
getting picked up as an illustration.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.200
And I should just hint,
out of this workflow

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.670
comes from leveraging the OCR,

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.630
optical character recognition data.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:54.210
So when all these books are scanned

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.280
and the text is attempted to be scanned,

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.970
that generates some
estimates, pretty noisy

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:01.460
of where there might be pictorial regions.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.910
So part of the use of deep
learning for this project

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:08.910
was to pop on a few classifiers
at the end of that pipeline

00:06:09.346 --> 00:06:14.210
to eliminate these false
positive, or junk images.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:17.250
And MHL was actually
employing student workers

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.870
to go through on Flickr
and delete these manually

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:22.450
which was very inefficient.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:26.650
And this is the paradigmatic
case for sort of automation

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:29.730
through machine learning is eliminating

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:33.630
these highly redundant
non-enriching tasks.

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:34.810
Here's some more of that junk.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.050
So you see they do pick
up some of the good stuff,

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:38.700
but it's more false positive.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.090
They're getting a lot of junk.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:41.940
And so then by the time

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.210
I was able to add some filtering to it,

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:46.213
we have much nicer results.

00:06:46.213 --> 00:06:47.750
At then end if we have time,

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:52.475
I can scroll through a little
bit of MHL's Flickr feeds.

00:06:52.475 --> 00:06:54.890
All their marks that have been made today

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:58.900
about ethics and privacy
apply incredibly more so

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:02.884
even to this archive because
you have a lot of ...

00:07:02.884 --> 00:07:04.480
The archive is defined as

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.600
sort of by the bodies of the deceased.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.713
And so questions of privacy
and ethics definitely come up.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.750
Okay, so existing work
on this space or in DH,

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:17.100
there's been a digital humanities,

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.860
there's been a lot of
probably the most work

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.560
on periodicals and newspapers.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.900
So the Library of Congress
has a big project,

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:26.140
The Europeana newspaper project,

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:27.940
the British Library has something,

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:30.400
to some nice papers that
have come out recently.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:31.780
This one by Wevers and Smits

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:35.140
uses retrain convolutional
neural networks.

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:37.360
And I'm glad all the
details of what those are

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:39.983
have been explained so capably before me.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.305
And what these teams have arrived at

00:07:44.305 --> 00:07:48.660
is some basic clustering or finding

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.070
of visual similarities, irregularities.

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:54.410
So Wevers and Smits
working in a Dutch archive,

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:56.680
found kind of all the
things in the newspaper

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.911
that were recurrent,
like the chess puzzles,

00:07:59.911 --> 00:08:02.591
or the weather patterns, or these clocks.

00:08:02.591 --> 00:08:05.340
And Paul Fyfe led a team kind of finding

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:07.280
the kind of incredible
whiteness as they called it

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:09.560
of the 19th century newspaper archive,

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:12.423
in the way it did it's sort of portraits.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.120
But there are problems with this.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:18.920
When you're working on
historical books or periodicals

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:20.946
as has been already shown before,

00:08:20.946 --> 00:08:25.946
the standard object recognition
engines are pretty bad.

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:27.440
Well, they don't give you what you want

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.270
because they are not
attuned to the materiality

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:33.850
of the book as a form,
and the different codes

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:38.230
and conventions of pictorial
representation within history,

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:40.973
within a particular print format.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:45.410
So here's some of the MHL
data thrown just yesterday

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:49.080
into Google and you get
sort of all these valance

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:52.500
and emotional scores.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:54.420
Yeah, I had where there
are certainly heads.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.011
But this doesn't capture anything

00:08:56.011 --> 00:08:58.900
about like the really awesome ...

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.200
I have some more examples from this text

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:02.480
which is on X-rays and stereoscopy.

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.630
But clearly this sort of
off the shelf solution

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:06.630
is not going to cut it.

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:09.303
You need to do a bit of
retraining in the domain.

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:15.050
Google also fails when
you have text and image,

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:18.860
which is sort of a the
dominant word image relation

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:21.440
with these inline images in the text,

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:22.687
within medical history.

00:09:22.687 --> 00:09:24.940
"Grey's Anatomy" actually 1830s and 40s

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:26.890
is one of the places where
you start to see a shift

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:31.590
from full page plate illustrations,
copper plate engravings,

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:34.080
things like that, to having smaller images

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.793
integrated into the text, often
made with wood engravings.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:42.560
So another problem with
projects in this DH

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.200
or cultural analytics space,

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:47.280
is that they often use data
that's already been annotated,

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:50.730
which again, doesn't really generalize

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:53.120
to the experience of that
individual researcher

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.163
or that small group
that needs to move fast

00:09:55.163 --> 00:09:57.700
and maybe doesn't have
that many resources,

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:00.120
whether of computing power or money.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:01.237
So you'll see at the bottom,

00:10:01.237 --> 00:10:04.190
a lot of these newspaper databases

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:07.730
have already been had
their layout annotated

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:10.940
with tools like DocWorks or Veridian.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:12.570
Doug has done some very clever things

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:13.610
so he knows his way around this.

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:16.400
But a lot of the work uses stuff

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:19.090
that's already been mapped out.

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:22.300
In other words, okay, it says
on this part of the page,

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:24.340
these coordinates there's a picture there,

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.230
in these parts there's a column of text.

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:28.740
Here's a headline, so on and so forth.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:31.033
But if you don't have that,
how're you gonna do it?

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:33.840
And the data on Internet Archive

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:35.510
does not come with that information.

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.180
You're kind of going blind.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.640
So I mentioned that the way to cut down

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:44.340
on the size of the problem
is to use the OCR data.

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:48.480
So here's a sample kind
of random book from MHL.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:50.520
It's online on the Internet Archive.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:54.730
And because Internet Archive
is open, MHL is extremely open,

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:55.950
polar tracing organization,

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:57.160
you can download anything you want,

00:10:57.160 --> 00:10:58.633
the raw images, whatever.

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.090
Here's what we kind of
do on our machine there.

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:06.390
So we get those initial estimates

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:09.240
of where the OCR thought,
okay this isn't text

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:10.660
that I will recognize.

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:11.663
This is a picture.

00:11:13.091 --> 00:11:17.760
And this has been done by
a guy named Kalev Leetaru.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:21.410
I worked this summer to try
to speed it up a little bit

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:23.840
and make it cleaner and incorporate

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.303
this sort of filtering step.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:27.760
Leetaru was approached
and some other people

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:29.980
have been to say, okay
the way we figure out

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:32.910
when we're analyzing historical
books, where our picture is

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:34.030
is we set all these rules.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:37.590
We say, well it can't be
less than 50 pixels wide.

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:40.160
And it can't come at the
beginning of the book,

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:43.280
and sort of all these
heuristics about the size,

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.000
the aspect ratio of the picture,

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:47.750
where it comes in the
sequence of page images.

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:50.490
And we wanted something more
generalizable than that,

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:55.120
that could find book
plates, illustrated covers,

00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:00.340
pictures of unusual size or aspect ratio.

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:02.190
And we wanted to filter out the junk.

00:12:03.553 --> 00:12:07.110
So this sort of leads
me to my first insight

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:10.900
is to sort of embrace the
labeling process itself.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:13.010
So when working with
cultural heritage data,

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:15.320
especially in the context of
these classification tasks,

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:18.270
and there's really no other
task other than classification.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.810
Any technical solution is gonna require

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:25.260
acts of historical
interpretation and comparison.

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:28.520
So then, noisy data,
like with MHL was facing,

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:31.370
is an opportunity I think,
to collaboratively learn

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:33.765
about historical ways of organizing

00:12:33.765 --> 00:12:36.060
and materializing knowledge.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:37.740
It's not a barrier to be overcome

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:41.126
through a very one time
clever engineering solution,

00:12:41.126 --> 00:12:44.440
or through hard coded heuristics.

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:46.552
So a corollary, we need
as many people involved

00:12:46.552 --> 00:12:51.552
in the data labeling and
annotating as possible.

00:12:51.711 --> 00:12:55.750
Ben Schmidt has made this kind
of a more NLP type project

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:58.080
but when you're working with data,

00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.750
and we were working mostly
with 19th century data,

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:01.590
well when you're working
with historical data

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:03.570
and classifiers,

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:05.230
often times what you're really looking at

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:07.610
is the history of, in many cases,

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:10.170
library classification systems themselves,

00:13:10.170 --> 00:13:13.830
or the way that the various people

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:17.200
and the sociology of the
text that produced the book

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:21.401
as an object conceived of the division

00:13:21.401 --> 00:13:24.630
and representation of knowledge.

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:26.810
So you're learning about
historical epistemology

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:28.063
as you do this work.

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:30.940
Okay, a very schematic view.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:34.400
There's a bunch of extracted
images from MHL in a cloud.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:36.480
I can thank Damon for actually.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.660
Here's a little logo
from this Diffgram tool

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:40.100
that'll hopefully show at the end

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:43.750
which provides a kind of way
to crowdsource annotation

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:46.330
for a small teams, individual researchers.

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:48.570
And there's a GPU pointing to the fact

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:49.990
that well, what do we have for MHL?

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:50.970
We have a little bit of stuff.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:53.770
We had a workstation with one GPU

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:55.110
and we had a few months to run it.

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:57.590
So that's kind of the basic setup.

00:13:57.590 --> 00:14:01.210
So by the numbers, we
started off by kind of doing

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:03.400
a long 19th century project.

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:07.140
So said that MHL had about 300K volumes,

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:08.790
a lot of them in the 19th century

00:14:09.922 --> 00:14:13.230
for obvious reasons having to
do with intellectual property.

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:16.550
So we ended up doing
pretty much the full set

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:20.880
of what MHL had for the 19th
century for 1770 to 1899,

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:23.930
123,000 volumes.

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:26.890
We extracted 2.2 million
in regions of interest,

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:28.540
that's just sort of neutral way,

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:29.800
I don't want to call it a picture

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:31.080
'cause in some cases it's not.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:32.810
You know there are some errors.

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:34.760
But they're visual regions of interest.

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:36.434
And I'll talk about more
how I trained the model

00:14:36.434 --> 00:14:41.434
and what I did when I was annotating,

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:43.319
bounding boxes around

00:14:43.319 --> 00:14:45.720
visual regions of interest
in historical books

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:47.090
and how I want to do that better,

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:49.840
and how I want all your input
on how to do that better.

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:54.763
Some facts about the speed of this.

00:14:56.142 --> 00:14:56.975
What did we use for this

00:14:56.975 --> 00:14:59.510
sort of transfer learning approach

00:15:00.483 --> 00:15:01.810
sitting at the end of this pipeline?

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:04.460
One of Google's MobileNet,
convolutional neural networks.

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:06.900
So it was a simple multinomial problem

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:08.621
I'll talk about in a little bit.

00:15:08.621 --> 00:15:10.770
There's all these
different classes of junk.

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:12.724
And then there's different
things that you want.

00:15:12.724 --> 00:15:15.330
And you apply this to the whole page image

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:16.400
then you keep the ones you want,

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:18.610
throw away the junk,
and then you use a Mask

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:23.610
R-CNN, localizing convolution neural net

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:26.000
to get the actual reasons that you want

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.583
and extract them from the page.

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:32.570
And okay in training, so I
did al the labeling myself.

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:38.560
And I think as I did about 3,000

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:39.967
before I got tired for the first stage,

00:15:39.967 --> 00:15:41.530
was just saying like hey does this page.

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:43.450
have an image on it, yes or no?

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.540
And about 300 for the next stage

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:48.250
which was saying, okay
here's a bunch of pages

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:49.760
with pictures on them,

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:51.580
draw a rectangle around where they are,

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:53.030
and then let the network,

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:55.663
which is trained on the
COCO dataset figure app,

00:15:57.808 --> 00:16:00.313
background class versus
region of interest class.

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:03.480
Here's a sort of schematic.

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:07.990
So here's a fun book by Matthew Bailey,

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:10.700
writer in the 1790s,
one of the first works

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:13.173
of pathological anatomy.

00:16:14.853 --> 00:16:17.640
So depictions of disease,
the etiology of it,

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.050
there's a kind of fun
literary history connection.

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:25.010
The illustrations for
this book shown here,

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:29.470
were in William and John
Hunter's Museum in London,

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:33.347
which was a kind of basis for the house in

00:16:33.347 --> 00:16:35.450
"Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde"
which is kind of cool.

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:37.800
Anyway, dig deep on any one book,

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:39.949
you'll find an amazing kind of thing.

00:16:39.949 --> 00:16:41.700
It's almost frustrating
how zoomed out this is,

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:43.879
but a necessary first step.

00:16:43.879 --> 00:16:44.890
Okay, so great.

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:47.390
So through the Internet Archive API

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:48.530
we can get this book.

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:52.540
It's 392 total pages, you know JPEGs.

00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:57.140
And then we use the OCR data to say,

00:16:57.140 --> 00:16:58.060
let's cut that on that.

00:16:58.060 --> 00:17:03.060
There's only 197 pages with a
likely illustration on them.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:06.420
And here's the sort of taxonomy of classes

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:08.300
that we came up with.

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:09.890
So we only want to keep certain classes.

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:11.120
Some are junk.

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:12.810
We're not really interested
in tables and charts,

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:15.160
although there's some cool DH projects

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:18.820
having to do with tabular
data and diagrams.

00:17:18.820 --> 00:17:21.130
We only want to keep our inline images,

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:24.720
plate images, and these they
were interested in book plates,

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:26.190
and end papers, and things like that.

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:28.620
So now we've cut down on the
space of the problem a lot.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:32.983
So we sort of have these
illustrated pages collected.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:36.900
Now what they wanted to do
with there Flickr project

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:40.270
was really have a kind
of crop or bounding box

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:41.270
of the images themselves.

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:43.170
So that's where the second
stage of this network,

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:45.450
this masked R-CNN came in.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:46.843
So you can now see.

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:48.930
And when I was training the data

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:52.027
I kind of just went like if
you were to crop this image,

00:17:52.027 --> 00:17:57.027
what would be the natural human
crop of a image in a book?

00:17:57.180 --> 00:17:59.521
So you want a little bit of white space.

00:17:59.521 --> 00:18:02.880
I went back and forth, I
wasn't super consistent

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:04.930
on whether to include captions.

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:07.010
That's the kind of like
very nitty gritty question

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:09.850
you probably want a group of
people to reach consensus on

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:11.910
and then go for it, which is why

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:13.598
I'm gonna show Diffgram at the end

00:18:13.598 --> 00:18:15.910
because it's a way of better achieving

00:18:15.910 --> 00:18:17.670
that kind of consensus.

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:19.770
So anyway, this is the pipeline.

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:22.410
And then you end up with 155
visual regions of interest.

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:25.170
And the nice thing about this
is that the Mask R-CNN model

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:28.310
can kind of decompose
complicated illustrations

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:31.250
that have maybe a couple
different parts to them

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:32.480
into the component parts,

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:36.903
but also able to keep in
many cases, the full diagram.

00:18:38.482 --> 00:18:40.940
Okay, we've talked about activation maps.

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:45.040
I don't need to do any material on that.

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:47.760
Here are some examples of
what was in the training data.

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:51.195
So the junk class contained
a lot of scanning artifacts.

00:18:51.195 --> 00:18:55.373
You see so many fingers and
hands and stuff it's great.

00:18:58.340 --> 00:18:59.495
And then the inline image class.

00:18:59.495 --> 00:19:02.360
We had great accuracy
and precision for this.

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:05.281
So these again are this
dominant 19th century

00:19:05.281 --> 00:19:07.470
sort of mode.

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:11.763
The wood engraving integrated
into the text of the page.

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:16.620
And I'll present a sort of
numbing number of figures here.

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:21.530
But this is just to show that
yep, even a simple problem

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:24.370
could require a bunch
of different classes.

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:25.890
And the key point is that you learn

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:28.040
while doing the analysis.

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:31.280
Like we got some things for
free doing this project.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:32.850
We now have a model that can predict,

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:37.293
oh that's a page of poetry,
or that's a page of text.

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:40.400
Here's some stuff

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:43.820
from the localization
part of the pipeline,

00:19:43.820 --> 00:19:46.930
the sort of loss or what you're aiming at

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:50.150
is a high IOU score which
is intersection over union.

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:52.160
So the brown there is the
union of the prediction,

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:55.460
and the ground truth label, and so forth.

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:57.043
Here's the tool I did it in.

00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:00.670
Importantly, this tool
just runs on your desktop.

00:20:00.670 --> 00:20:02.287
So I'm interested in showing Diffgram

00:20:02.287 --> 00:20:05.740
and the time I have remaining
here are some results.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:08.269
And it's a more collaborative tool.

00:20:08.269 --> 00:20:09.761
Well, what can we show?

00:20:09.761 --> 00:20:14.757
You can't really show
anything about visual themes

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:17.970
or specific objects at this scale.

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:19.790
We have millions of images.

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:21.030
I know the lab has been working on

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:22.380
putting them into PixPlot.

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:25.790
But you can show stuff
about the format of a book

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:29.440
at scale over a whole century
for all of medical publishing

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:30.960
in the West almost.

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:34.090
So you find that books
become way more illustrated.

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:37.480
So on average a book
would have illustrations

00:20:37.480 --> 00:20:39.560
about 1% of its pages at the
beginning of the century,

00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:41.660
but by the end it's about 6%.

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:42.860
And what's driving that?

00:20:42.860 --> 00:20:43.990
Well, the blue and the red lines,

00:20:43.990 --> 00:20:45.550
are very closely tide

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:47.680
and the increase is all coming

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:50.110
from these inline illustrations.

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:52.120
So publishers are
figuring how to integrate

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:54.000
illustration into the page.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:57.593
The expensive engraved
plates stay the same.

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:00.440
And here's a breakdown by city.

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:02.880
So okay, as I finish up.

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:05.350
What are we moving towards
next with this MHL project?

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:08.093
Well, the age of photography at scale.

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:13.330
And we're gonna try to have
more focused experiments

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:18.330
on distinguishing different
types of photographic processes,

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:19.820
considering more thoroughly

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:22.766
these legal and ethical questions,

00:21:22.766 --> 00:21:24.480
thinking about advertising.

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:26.270
Advertising starts to
come into these books.

00:21:26.270 --> 00:21:28.183
And then representations of disease.

00:21:30.187 --> 00:21:33.550
Okay, so I just wanted to
show you what I'm using now,

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:35.560
which is called Diffgram,

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:37.913
which uses a forward annotation.

00:21:43.170 --> 00:21:44.003
Oh yeah, good.

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:46.330
This is just a picture from Diffgram

00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:47.284
which is good.

00:21:47.284 --> 00:21:50.820
So okay, so Diffgram allows
you to collaboratively label.

00:21:50.820 --> 00:21:53.420
And you can get some nice
hierarchical labeling.

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:57.163
So we're just in this unit of the figure.

00:21:58.580 --> 00:21:59.900
So that includes the capture

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:01.390
and it includes the whole thing.

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:04.260
But a lot of books in the first
decades of the 19th century

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:06.670
have stereoscopic views,

00:22:06.670 --> 00:22:08.250
so you have left view and the right view

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:09.520
and you use the viewer.

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:11.850
So that in itself is a
very interesting category.

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:16.510
So I label the two as a
pair, as a stereoscopic pair,

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:19.160
and then I also label
each of these as an extra.

00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:21.630
And that's the kind of
collaborative sort of

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:23.050
nested hierarchical labeling

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:25.810
that you can only do with
one of these new frameworks.

00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:27.840
The cool thing about Diffgram
is that as you label,

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:30.450
it starts to propose
better labels for you.

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:33.840
So I'm interested in what's
out there in the space.

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:35.590
And I'd love to talk after this.

00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:36.580
But thank you so much for your time.

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:37.894
- [Moderator] Thank you, very much.

00:22:37.894 --> 00:22:40.894
(audience applauds)

00:22:43.621 --> 00:22:46.204
(gentle music)

